<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm Frick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm Frick was a leading Nazi politician. In 1930 he became interior minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and from 1933 to 1943 he was Reich Minister of the Interior, drafting laws that centralised the state and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> race laws. He was hanged after the Nuremberg trials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-frick.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Frick" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frick_912_(3x4_cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nazi writers such as <b>Wilhelm Frick</b> and Alfred Rosenberg labelled it “un-German”, stirred controversy over flat roofs and painted it as a front for communists, and each move and change of leadership followed political pressure.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 <b>Wilhelm Frick</b> became Thuringia's interior minister, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alsenz, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Nuremberg Court Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.73,7.81&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Alsenz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4729 432 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wilhelm Frick"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5179 702h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5179 702h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.73,7.81&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
